{
  "image_name": "7dfdce66c10a2d55d141f0ae0327d2d7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how major industrial materials are made and how their carbon flows differ, using cement, steel and petrochemicals as the main examples. At the top, a Sankey-style diagram links raw inputs such as limestone, silica, iron ore, coke, bauxite and fossil fuel feedstocks to transformed products including cement, steel, aluminium and petrochemicals. It emphasizes where carbon ends up: some material goes “to stocks” in long-lived products, while a large share is shown as lost during production or at end of life. A blue loop indicates end-of-life recycling, which seems important for metals but limited for petrochemical products.\n\nThe middle section sketches the core process chains. Cement is made from limestone and clay through preheating, precalcining and clinker formation, followed by grinding and storage. Steel production proceeds from coal, limestone and iron ore through a blast furnace, then a basic oxygen furnace and casting. Polyethylene production starts from ethane, then monomer production and purification, catalytic polymerization, and final separation, solvent recovery, drying and pelletization.\n\nAt the bottom, simplified reaction equations show why these sectors emit CO2: calcination in cement, ore reduction in steel, and hydrocarbon cracking/polymerization in polyethylene manufacture.",
